WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.633
How you should hold your mouse is a bit of a tricky topic to talk about

00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:07.162
It's something I've seen so many people overthink to their detriment 

00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:10.069
and yet I entirely understand why they do it

00:00:10.069 --> 00:00:13.216
Because mouse grip does dictate a lot about how someone aims

00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:16.622
but it also doesnt really matter, it's not worth overthinking

00:00:16.622 --> 00:00:21.689
To explain this contradiction though, I need to go over why people overthink it in the first place

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:25.950
There are three main ways to hold a mouse, palm, claw and fingertip.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.390
These categories won't cover every little detail of your grip style but the two main things

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:35.033
that separate these three are the most relevant ones for how your grip impacts your aim

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.457
palm contact and finger mobility

00:00:37.457 --> 00:00:42.102
Looking at a standard palm grip you can see that the entire palm is fully pressed against the mouse

00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:45.267
with the index and middle fingers laying flat along the top

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:49.246
This is actually how I used to grip, back then I played a lot of counter strike

00:00:49.246 --> 00:00:55.285
and having the mouse totally locked in place helps with stability which is extremely important for winning duels in that game

00:00:55.285 --> 00:01:00.245
When I started to pick up other games though I ended up raising my sensitivity and on that higher sens

00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:03.750
I noticed that I was struggling to make smaller corrections when aiming

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.002
While the stability offered by palm grip was nice, it came with a tradeoff

00:01:08.002 --> 00:01:11.144
When aiming with palm grip, you lose the ability to move the mouse with

00:01:11.144 --> 00:01:14.321
your fingers and limit yourself to just wrist and arm

00:01:14.321 --> 00:01:20.617
and that wasn't too big of a deal in counter strike, I played really slow sensitivityand my arm and wrist were precise enough

00:01:20.617 --> 00:01:24.787
but as I raised my sens and played games that required more vertical movement specifically

00:01:24.787 --> 00:01:27.578
I felt that this grip was holding me back

00:01:27.578 --> 00:01:31.640
That's when I did some looking around online and found people using fingertip grip

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:37.116
coming from palm, this looked so unnatural! but the thought process behind it made sense

00:01:37.116 --> 00:01:40.519
The idea was that by creating that clearance between the palm and the mouse

00:01:40.519 --> 00:01:45.085
there was enough room to move the mouse vertically exclusively with your fingertips

00:01:45.085 --> 00:01:49.625
This gave a level of fine control vertically that just wasn't possible with palm grip

00:01:49.625 --> 00:01:56.866
and since being able to use your fingertips could help make and stabilize horizontal movements as well, it seemed like the obvious choice

00:01:56.866 --> 00:02:02.704
With my new higher sensitivity I needed that precision for smaller movements and I decided to make the swap

00:02:02.704 --> 00:02:06.503
Personally though, no matter how much time and practice I put into this grip style

00:02:06.503 --> 00:02:08.957
I couldn't get over it's downsides

00:02:08.957 --> 00:02:14.458
When making wider flicks with my arm I felt that I had to apply a lot of pressure to the mouse to keep it firmly in place

00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:19.018
To achieve this I put more tension in my fingers, tension that I couldn't quickly release

00:02:19.018 --> 00:02:21.863
after a flick which made my microadjustments shakier

00:02:21.863 --> 00:02:28.246
It created a disconnect between small and large movements and I found myself just using my wrist instead

00:02:28.246 --> 00:02:32.277
So if both extremes have tradeoffs, why not try to strike a balance?

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:37.664
Claw grip is characterised by having the base of the palm touching while the fingers on top of the mouse curl

00:02:37.664 --> 00:02:43.357
This lets it provide some vertical finger mobility while still having a bit of the stability benefits that palm grip offers

00:02:43.357 --> 00:02:49.241
and it mostly avoids the tradeoffs of the other two grips but it can't provide their full benefits either

00:02:49.241 --> 00:02:52.990
This is what ended up choosing, it works well for me for the most part, more on that later

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.853
but for some people striking that middle ground just isn't good enough

00:02:56.853 --> 00:03:01.123
They might play on a higher sensitivity, and claw grip just doesnt offer the control 

00:03:01.123 --> 00:03:04.519
they need to remain precise on that sens, and on a sens that high

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:09.796
you don't need to make those large, cross mousepad movements anyways so why not use fingertip?

00:03:09.796 --> 00:03:13.917
and even though I wouldn't recommend it most of the time, palm grip still cant work

00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:17.694
If your sensitivity is low enough you probably don't need much fingertip mobility

00:03:17.694 --> 00:03:21.000
but those stability benefits could win you gunfights

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.288
This also explains how the concept of the "perfect mouse grip" is fundamentally flawed

00:03:26.288 --> 00:03:28.769
Since each grip has different benefits and weaknesses

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:31.676
which is best depends not only on the main game someone plays

00:03:31.676 --> 00:03:34.432
but also on their sensitivity and handsize

00:03:34.432 --> 00:03:36.613
There is no universal best grip.

00:03:37.679 --> 00:03:41.721
But if all of that is true, how can mouse grip not matter?

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:45.640
I spent so much time experimenting to find what worked best after swapping games

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.098
and eventually I did settle on claw, right?

00:03:49.098 --> 00:03:52.317
For the most part yes, and there is nothing wrong with experimenting

00:03:52.317 --> 00:03:55.914
especially since none of these grips are objectively the best for aiming

00:03:55.914 --> 00:03:59.200
but it's not worth overthinking because of two key concepts

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.252
comfort and adaptability, which sounds confusing but by adaptability

00:04:03.252 --> 00:04:06.720
I just mean that your grip will naturally change based on what you are doing

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.956
This is why I don't say that I've fully settled on a grip style

00:04:09.956 --> 00:04:12.774
if you watch my hand as I play you'll see a lot of details about 

00:04:12.774 --> 00:04:15.729
how I hold the mouse change based on the situation

00:04:15.729 --> 00:04:17.801
An example of this is that when I flick

00:04:17.801 --> 00:04:20.804
I put more downwards pressure on the mouse to increase my stopping power

00:04:20.804 --> 00:04:23.838
but there are more extreme changes as well like swapping to fingertip grip

00:04:23.838 --> 00:04:27.534
when I know I'm going to make use of that vertical freedom of movement

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:31.527
This is entirely subconscious and from what I've seen is fairly common

00:04:31.527 --> 00:04:36.150
most people I've asked do the same and this is reflected in a poll I put up on twitter (but said in a very aussie accent)

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.893
but then, why didn't I naturally swap from palm grip in the past?

00:04:39.893 --> 00:04:43.891
I had to consciously think about different options and choose to try them

00:04:43.891 --> 00:04:46.949
That's because I left out a very important detail earlier

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:53.102
at the time I was using the... logitech g502, a very heavy mouse designed for palm grip

00:04:53.102 --> 00:04:58.158
The weight prevented me from comfortably moving it with my fingers and it was way too big for me to claw

00:04:58.158 --> 00:05:02.891
I got a new mouse when I tried to swap to fingertip grip and it's what made that switch possible for me

00:05:02.891 --> 00:05:06.991
but going from fingertip to claw after that was entirely subconscious

00:05:06.991 --> 00:05:10.883
I didn't think about it at all and just naturally found what worked best for me

00:05:10.883 --> 00:05:15.278
by not forcing myself, or having a mouse force me into a certain style

00:05:15.278 --> 00:05:18.077
This leads nicely into what I think is the most important 

00:05:18.077 --> 00:05:21.150
element of how you hold your mouse, comfort

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.875
You won't feel the benefits of any of these grip styles

00:05:23.875 --> 00:05:26.874
if they aren't comfortable for you or compatible with your current mouse

00:05:26.874 --> 00:05:29.912
and while it might be tempting to try and force a grip style

00:05:29.912 --> 00:05:33.521
to match how someone else holds the mouse or because you think itll help your aim

00:05:33.521 --> 00:05:37.495
if it's causing you pain or just isn't comfortable it's not worth doing

00:05:37.495 --> 00:05:41.864
Most of the time the grip you naturally gravitate towards when putting your hand on the mouse

00:05:41.864 --> 00:05:46.086
is what's going to work best for you, it's how I ended up with my current grip style

00:05:46.086 --> 00:05:50.031
But then, what if how you hold the mouse naturally is causing you pain?

00:05:50.031 --> 00:05:52.477
Then it makes sense to try other options in terms of grip

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:56.607
but you might find that your current mouse just doesn't work for your hand

00:05:56.607 --> 00:06:02.226
As obvious as it sounds I didn't realise for a long time that holding a mouse isn't meant to be painful

00:06:02.226 --> 00:06:05.032
and if no grip style feels comfortable it might be worth

00:06:05.032 --> 00:06:08.402
looking into getting a mouse that better suits your handsize

00:06:08.402 --> 00:06:12.234
This is one of two situations where I think it makes sense to

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:14.013
think about your grip consciously

00:06:14.013 --> 00:06:16.543
If your hand hurts or it doesn't feel comfortable

00:06:16.543 --> 00:06:18.823
try different grips and see if that helps

00:06:18.823 --> 00:06:21.183
The other situation is a lot more mechanical

00:06:21.183 --> 00:06:24.521
if you're noticing when watching your vods back that you're consistently struggling

00:06:24.521 --> 00:06:28.521
to make small vertical microadjustments and you use a palm or

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:30.667
sometimes it can happen with claw grip too,

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:33.776
you might not be able to engage your fingers enough when aiming

00:06:33.776 --> 00:06:36.404
Without being able to microadjust with your fingertips you have to use

00:06:36.404 --> 00:06:41.238
the next closest relevant muscle, which is the shoulder and it is nowhere near as accurate

00:06:41.238 --> 00:06:45.771
In either of these situations I think experimenting with grip style is beneficial

00:06:45.771 --> 00:06:48.383
 but I still wouldn't overthink it 

00:06:48.383 --> 00:06:51.602
In my opinion it's never worth it to hyperfocus on the

00:06:51.602 --> 00:06:54.065
specific locations of all your fingers or try to force 

00:06:54.065 --> 00:06:56.576
the exact same grip each time you play

00:06:56.576 --> 00:07:00.317
Grip style is a lot more malleable than people think and it's totally normal

00:07:00.317 --> 00:07:03.950
for it to change from day to day or even throughout a playsession

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:07.222
I think the most important thing to remember when discussing things like this

00:07:07.222 --> 00:07:12.061
is that they should always be secondary to practicing and focusing on weaknesses

00:07:12.061 --> 00:07:15.915
Most of the benefits of swapping grip style, especially for smaller changes

00:07:15.915 --> 00:07:17.661
don't come without any tradeoffs

00:07:17.661 --> 00:07:21.932
This is true for most of these so called "instant improvement methods" honestly

00:07:21.932 --> 00:07:24.560
It's very easy to fall into the trap of thinking that

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.629
to get better, you just need to find the secret mouse grip

00:07:27.629 --> 00:07:30.706
or setting or sensitivity and so on and so on

00:07:30.706 --> 00:07:33.794
While changing one of these *might* help in extreme cases

00:07:33.794 --> 00:07:37.534
most of the time it won't make a significant difference and at the end of the day

00:07:37.534 --> 00:07:40.970
the biggest defining factor for how good you are is the time you put in

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.944
not your gripstyle.

